## Business Goals Constraints

- A bundle is published once and installed by any org that acquires it, on any of its zones, without that org building anything.
- One bundle can carry realm components (on every chosen realm of the zone) and zone components (once on the zone).
- Component groups let a buyer turn parts of a bundle on and off per installation.
- Releases are numbered `1, 2, 3…`; a version identifies a release, tags (`latest`, `stable`, …) point at versions.
- Auto-update keeps installations current with releases the publisher marked compatible; deprecations and security advisories move installations off old versions on a schedule.
- A buyer can take a component's source into their own repository and run their build in place of the bundle's (an override), and give it back later.
- Installing is planned and applied by hash, so a change nobody reviewed is never applied.

## Performance Constraints

| Limit | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Source archive files per component | 999 | `BundleBuild.MaxSourceFiles` / `SourceArchives.MaxFiles` |
| Source file size | 10 MB | `BundleBuild.MaxSourceFileBytes` |
| Source total size | 25 MB − 64 KB | `BundleBuild.MaxSourceTotalBytes` |
| Source archive (compressed) | 32 MB | `BundleBuild.MaxSourceArchiveBytes` |
| Source path length | 640 | `BundleBuild.MaxSourcePathLength` |
| Release / catalog page size (server max) | 200 | `BundleService.MaxPageSize` |
| Installation history page (server max) | 200 | `InstallationService.MaxHistoryPage` |
| Source delivery list page (server max) | 100 | `SourceDeliveries.MaxListPage` |
| Notices per bundle, resolved ones included | 100 | `BundleService.MaxNotices` |
| Notice message length | 2000 | `BundleService.MaxNoticeMessageLength` |
| Deprecation lead time when other orgs run the versions (end of support from now, end of life after end of support) | 30 days each | `BundleService.NoticeLeadTime` |
| Security advisory update-by lead time when other orgs run the versions | 7 days | `BundleService.SecurityLeadTime` |
| Upload URL lifetime | 1 hour | `MarketplaceStore.UploadUrlExpirationSeconds` |
| Auto-update sweep period | 10 min | `AutomaticUpdates` options |
| `bundles install` re-plans after a 409 (interactive only) | 3 attempts | `InstallBundleCommand.Run` |

Never in a source archive: `.git`, `bin`, `obj`, `node_modules`, `.beamable/temp`, `.beamable/local`, `.wingman`. Archives are deterministic (fixed timestamps), so unchanged source keeps its hash and isn't uploaded again.

## Usage Guide

Always pass `-q` from an agent: `publish`, `install`, `uninstall` and `acquire` otherwise wait for a typed `yes`.

### Names

- The full name is `@<namespace>/<name>`; the namespace is the customer's cid alias, derived at runtime. Commands that take `bundle-name` accept the short name for your own bundles and need the full name for anyone else's.
- The short name is the config file's stem: `<name>.beam.bundle.json`. It holds no name field.

### Bundle config file

`bundles new <name> -c <beamoId>…` writes `<name>.beam.bundle.json`:

```json
{
  "components": {
    "Scores":        { "source": true },
    "ScoresStorage": { "source": true },
    "ScoresPanel":   { "site": "zone" }
  },
  "componentGroups": {
    "default": { "title": "Scores", "description": "", "components": ["Scores", "ScoresStorage", "ScoresPanel"], "enabledByDefault": true }
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@acme/players": { "kind": "hard", "min": 2, "max": 5 }
  }
}
```

| Field | Rule |
|---|---|
| `components.<beamoId>.site` | Optional `realm` or `zone`; must equal the project's scope (`BeamServiceScope`, or `beamable.serviceScope` in an extension's `package.json`) |
| `components.<beamoId>.source` | Buyers may request this component's source. A service with `source` requires `source` on its storages |
| `componentGroups` | Required when there are components; every component in at least one group; no unknown or repeated names |
| `dependencies.<@ns/name>` | Full name; `kind` `hard` (must be installed, in range) or `soft` (suggested); `min` ≥ 0; `max` optional, ≥ `min` |

Checked against the projects before plan and publish: services, storages and portal extensions only; a service's storages are in the bundle, on the same site, and in every group that has the service; a beamoId is in at most one bundle; bundle names are unique. Unknown or old properties are rejected with the file named.
{{ command_tables "bundles new" }}

### Publish

```
beam_exec("bundles create scores --visibility private -q")     # once: claims the name
beam_exec("bundles plan scores -q")                             # build + diff vs latest
beam_exec("bundles publish scores --from-latest-plan --tag stable -q")
```

- `plan` builds realm and zone components separately and reports each change (`Added`, `Removed`, `Changed`) by `kind`: `service`, `storage`, `extension`, `schema`, `componentGroup`, `dependency`. `isNoOp` when nothing changed.
- `publish` asks the server for upload targets, pushes service images to the marketplace repos it names, uploads only the files it doesn't have, then posts the release. `isNewVersion` is false when the content equals the latest release's.
- `--compatible-since <component>=<version>` marks a changed component compatible with installs from that version, so auto-update can apply the release; a changed component without one counts as breaking. `--irreversible-data-change` marks the release as unsafe to roll back.
- `create`, `update`, `notice *` and `yank` need an org admin; `publish` and `tag` need a developer. Only the owning org changes a bundle (`BundleNotOwned`).
{{ command_tables "bundles create" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles update" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles plan" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles publish" }}

### Releases, tags, yank, notices

```
beam_exec("bundles releases scores -q")                 # versions, tags, notices
beam_exec("bundles releases scores --version 4 -q")     # plus v4's components, groups, dependencies
beam_exec("bundles tag scores stable 4 -q")
beam_exec("bundles yank scores 3 -q")
beam_exec("bundles notice create scores --min-version 1 --max-version 3 --end-of-support 2027-01-15 --end-of-life 2027-03-01 --message \"Move to v4\" --replacement-version 4 -q")
beam_exec("bundles notice create scores --kind security-advisory --min-version 2 --max-version 3 --severity high --advisory-id CVE-2026-1234 --replacement-version 4 --update-by 2027-01-01 --message \"Token leak in Scores\" -q")
beam_exec("bundles notice update scores <notice id> --resolve -q")
```

- Yank blocks new installs of a version (`BundleVersionYanked`); installations running it keep it.
- A notice has a `--kind` (default `deprecation`); the kind decides its options, and the server refuses options the kind doesn't take (`BundleNoticeInvalid`). Every notice needs `--message`.

| Kind | Covers | Options | What it does |
|---|---|---|---|
| `deprecation` | Versions | `--end-of-support`, `--end-of-life`, replacement | Sets the versions' status: `supported` → `deprecated` (notice saved) → `unsupported` (after end of support: no new installs) → `endoflife` (installations are updated off it). One per version |
| `security-advisory` | Versions | `--severity` (required), `--advisory-id`, the fix as replacement, `--update-by` (needs the fix) | Warns like a deprecation. `critical` refuses new installs. After update-by, installations move to the fix or later. Auto-update never moves onto a version with an unresolved advisory |
| `known-issue` | Versions | The fix as replacement | Warns only |
| `retirement` | Whole bundle | `--acquisitions-end` | After that date nothing can acquire it and it leaves search and placements; installations keep running. One per bundle |
| `announcement` | Versions or whole bundle | `--link` | Shown with the bundle (listing, releases, library, installations), never as a warning, in plans, deploys or `bundles notices` |

- Leave out both `--min-version` and `--max-version` for a whole-bundle notice. A notice's kind and versions never change; `notice update` only moves dates later.
- With other orgs on the versions: a deprecation's end of support is at least 30 days out and its end of life at least 30 days after that; an advisory's update-by is at least 7 days out.
- `notice update --resolve` resolves a security advisory, known issue or announcement: it stops warning and acting, stays in the timeline, and can't be edited or reopened. Deprecations and retirements are withdrawn instead (`notice withdraw`), which works on any kind.
- In output, every notice has `kind`, `warns` (unresolved and not an announcement) and a one-line `description`. Only a deprecation sets a version's `status`. Installations and library entries carry `notice` (the deprecation) and `notices` (every unresolved notice on the installed version).
{{ command_tables "bundles releases" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles tag" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles yank" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles notice create" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles notice update" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles notice withdraw" }}

### Catalog and marketplace

`bundles list` shows the workspace's bundles and your org's catalog entries (private included). `search`, `info` and `placements` read public bundles; `placements` finds Beamable-listed bundles that mount into a portal page or extension.
{{ command_tables "bundles list" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles search" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles info" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles placements" }}

### Library

A zone can only install bundles the org owns or acquired. `acquire` checks first: hard dependencies must already be in the library (`missingHardDependencies`), soft ones are only suggested, and `warnings` carries notices such as a retirement. A retired bundle can't be acquired after its acquisitions end. `acquire` and `remove` need a customer admin (`CustomerAdminRequired`); `remove` is refused while the bundle is installed anywhere.
{{ command_tables "bundles acquire" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles remove" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles library" }}

### Install, change, uninstall

```
beam_exec("bundles install @acme/scores --plan-only -q")                     # review
beam_exec("bundles install @acme/scores --plan-hash <hash> -q")              # apply exactly that plan
beam_exec("bundles install @acme/scores --tag stable --component-groups default,leaderboards --realms all --auto-update true -q")
beam_exec("bundles uninstall @acme/scores -q")
```

- The same command installs and changes; omitted options keep the installed values (or defaults on a new install: latest, groups with `enabledByDefault`, all realms, the org's auto-update setting). `--component-groups=` with no value turns every group off.
- The plan lists per manifest (zone, then each realm) `add`, `upgrade`, `archive`, `keep` (an override stays) or `detach`, plus `refusals` and dependency `blockers`; either stops the apply.
- Apply sends the plan's `hash`. If anything moved since, the server answers 409 (`InstallationPlanChanged`, `ManifestChanged`): with `-q` or `--plan-hash` nothing is applied and the command fails; interactively it re-plans and asks again.
- Each manifest reports `applied`, `pending` or `failed`; running the same command again retries the ones left.
- Uninstall archives the components; storage data is kept. `--detach <component>` keeps an overridden component as the org's own instead of archiving it.
- `--target-zone <zid>` acts on another zone of the org; the default is the workspace's zone.
- Plans warn about every notice on the target version except announcements. Moving onto a version past its end of support, or under a critical security advisory, is refused.
- `bundles notices` lists the org's installations, on every zone, once per notice that warns (deprecations, security advisories, known issues, retirements).
{{ command_tables "bundles install" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles uninstall" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles installations" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles history" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles dependencies" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles notices" }}

### Source and overrides

```
beam_exec("bundles source request @acme/scores Scores --repository <connection id> -q")
beam_exec("bundles source list -q")      # state: requested, working, opened, pushed, failed
```

The delivery is a pull request (made with the requester's token) into a repository connected to the zone. Each delivered project gets a `beam.override.json` at its root:

```json
{ "bundle": "@acme/scores", "version": 4, "component": "Scores", "connectionId": "<guid>", "kind": "service" }
```

`deploy plan` / `deploy release` from that checkout deploy the project as an override of the installed component. Before building anything the CLI checks, and lists every failure at once:

| Check | Failure |
|---|---|
| The file parses and has `bundle`, `version`, `component`, `connectionId` | `needs bundle, version, component and connectionId` |
| `component` equals the project's beamoId | the project was renamed |
| `kind`, when given, matches the project (services and portal extensions only) | kind mismatch |
| The project is in a Git checkout with a remote (`upstream`, else `origin`) | not in a Git checkout with a remote |
| The connection is one of the zone's | isn't one of this zone's repository connections |
| You have a token for the connection or its host | no token |
| The checkout's remote is the connection's repository (host/path compared, any transport, without `.git`) | remote belongs to another repository |
| The bundle is installed on the site with that component, and no other repository overrides it | nothing to override / already overridden |

Server-side refusals: `OverrideRefused`, `OverriddenComponentLocked` (a bundle change can't touch an overridden component), `BundleComponentNameClash`. `bundles installations` shows each override with its `baseVersion` and `changedIn` (a newer release changed the component).

Give a component back with `bundles end-override <bundle> <component>` (current realm, or `--zone`), or with `deploy … --end-override <component>`.
{{ command_tables "bundles source request" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles source list" }}
{{ command_tables "bundles end-override" }}

### Errors

| Code | Status | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `BundleNameTaken` | 409 | `create` on an existing name |
| `BundleNameNotFound` | 404 | No such bundle, or not visible to your org |
| `BundleNamespaceViolation` | 403 | Publishing outside your org's namespace |
| `BundleNotOwned` | 403 | Changing another org's bundle |
| `BundleVersionNotFound` / `BundleVersionYanked` | 404 / 400 | Version doesn't exist / is yanked |
| `BundleTagInvalid` | 400 | Tags: lowercase letters, digits, `-`, `_`, starting with a letter or digit |
| `BundleDepRangeInvalid` / `BundleDepRangeUnresolvable` | 400 | Bad range / a bound the dependency never released |
| `BundleDepNotPublic` / `BundleDepCycle` / `BundleDepSelfReference` | 400 | Dependency not public / cyclic / itself |
| `BundleArtifactsInvalid` / `BundleContentInvalid` | 400 | Uploaded artifacts don't match the release / bad release content |
| `BundleRepositoryTaken` | 409 | A component's marketplace repo belongs to another org |
| `BundleNoticeInvalid` / `BundleNoticeChanged` | 400 / 409 | Notice dates or fields invalid / edited concurrently |
| `AcquisitionRefused` / `BundleNotAcquired` | 400 / 404 | Can't acquire / install needs the bundle in the library |
| `InstallationRefused` | 400 | The plan has refusals or blockers |
| `InstallationPlanChanged` / `ManifestChanged` | 409 | Something moved since the plan or the manifest was read |
| `ZoneScopeRequired` | 400 | The request needs a zone scope |
| `CustomerAdminRequired` | 403 | Library changes need a customer admin |
